A||||12||mikemrq||vapor canister||||1||
Z||000000||mikemrq||06-02-2001||07:41 PM||||on my '86 fiero, v6, 4sp. the filter on the bottom of the vapor canister is verry dirty. could not find a place that carries them or the canister. Had to make a filter out of 1/4" felt. How can i tell if my vapor canister is working ok? if it must be replaced, where can i get one?||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||06-02-2001||10:10 PM||||If your vapor canister isn't leaking out raw gas, then it is fine.  All it does is collect fumes from the tank, and then stores them until the car is started up next.  When vacuum is applied to the valve at the top, it purges all the gas into the intake manifold to aid in starting up the car.<P>If you really want to replace it, I know the fiero store carries a new one for about $50 US  <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000002||c m west||06-02-2001||11:32 PM||||or just place the vapor canister into the nearest trashcan and run the vacum tube to the cruise control - done deal<P><BR>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero_24||06-03-2001||10:51 AM||||You will know if it is not working by turning the car on at night and look at the engine.  The key to look at is the exhaust manifold(s).  If they glow red you are in trouble, if not you are OK.  I have had to change this canister in every Fiero I have bought.  I go to Autozone for my canisters.  $34.99, but they have to be ordered.  For a quick fix if it happens on the road, unplug the hose that goes to the intake area and plug it with something (I use an old spark plug).  The reason it glows red is because the purge valve on the canister goes bad and starts sucking fuel instead of air.<P>Craig||||reg||1
Z||000004||drednot||06-03-2001||01:08 PM||||c m west-- how does that work??||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||06-03-2001||03:25 PM||||From data that will be used to update my emmisions article....<P><BR>The filter for the bottom of the canister is FRAM # BA3865 and is available anyplace that has Fram. I get them at Wall Mart. They are less than $2.00. You may have to order them from some places.<P>The canister fails one of a couple ways. The purge valve quits or the carbon grains get fuel soaked by liquid gas. In either case the canister is shot and has to be replaced.<P>The purge control line is the smaller line. This line should hold a small amount of vacuum indefinatly. (Don't pull more than a few inches of vac or you'll damage the valve. This line never sees full manfifold vac. One squeeze on the hand pump is penty.)<P>The Larger vac line is the actual purge line. This line should hold vacuum untill a small amount of vacuum is applied to the control line. At that point it will flow air out of the canister. It should close tightly as soon as vac drops on the control line.<P>You can get these canisters most places. I think they might be in the MotorMight Master Catalog... that thing has all sorts of not common stuff.<P>There are 3 reasons to make sure this canister works.<P>1. It's required under U.S. Law. If the canister is missing you will not pass inspection in many places. (Delaware being one... Here the canister is physically inspected as part of the gas tank test. Missing/disconnected canister in instant fail.) Service Shops that remove this device face huge fines.... The kind that can out you out of business.<P>2. A working canister actually saves you money on gas. The fumes are trapped and fed to your engine instead of the air.<P>3. Leaving the tank vent open to the air creates a fire hazard. It could cause an explosion hazard.<P><BR>Joe,,, The canister does not purge durring startup. The control line is connected to a vacuum port up stream (ahead) of the throttle plate. the purge line doesn't get any vacuum untill the throttle opens above idle by a certain amount. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||c m west||06-03-2001||04:41 PM||||the vacum canister is designed to hold vacum for power assisted accessories such as power brakes, however the Fiero does not run a loppy-wicked cam and does not need the additional support of a vacum canister||||reg||1
Z||000007||c m west||06-03-2001||04:44 PM||||I take back everything I ssaid - I thought we were talking about the VACUM canister below the cruise control soleniod, and not the VAPOR canister<P> so I am wrong and disregard everything I said ( I hate it when I have a brain fart )||||reg||1
