Z||000009||ka4nkf||06-03-2001||09:32 PM||||theogre<BR>You stated that the tank vent line ran to the canister. My question is, I have a lot of trouble filling my gas tank. I have to let the last gallon or so go in real slow or it keeps cutting off the nozzle. Could my canister be messed up? I changed the filter and the canister looks good and no smell of gas.<BR>thanks<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||06-04-2001||12:17 PM||||I doubt the canister is causing a filling problem. There is a seconary vent line up to the filler opening that handles this high volume ventilation.<P>You may simply be over filling the tank. Keep in mind many gauges are off to start with or off because of sender wear... This can lead you to think the tank has less than it really does inside.<P>The tank capacity is listed such that there will be some room left for fuel expansion. The tanks are not supposed to be stuffed in any car. ever. The kind of filling you are indicating often means your gauge is off or some such thing.<P>To get the real capacity tested, wait untill the tank is low then use a thin hose to suck it dry. Soft surgical or IV tubing with a tiny bit of weight on it would work. Any tubing that will be soft enough to reach the bottom and stay there... You want to pull out every drop you can get. (Best way to weight the tube is with a small section copper or steel tube inside the plastic. (An inch of hard line will usually do.) That way the weight won't get caught on something in the tank comming out...)<P>Once that's done, pour in a Measured Amount of gas so you can get to the filling station. Measuer carefully! it's important. <P>Go to the filling station that is closest. You don't want to burn any more of this fuel than needed. Ideally you should have enough gas in cans handy that you don't need to drive at all and can just fill it from the cans. (Meausering carefully as you do...) <P>At the station, Add ONLY enough fuel to get to the tanks rated capacity as listed for that year/model.<P>This amount should go into the tank without trouble.<P>Note where the measured fill pushed the gas gauge to. This is your Full Mark reguardless of what the marks on the gauge read.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Devotshka||06-04-2001||07:14 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>lead you to think the tank has less than it really does inside.<P>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-04-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmm.... i can never get enough gas into my car either, but i even went to such lengths as to drop the fuel tank and drain it the hard way (or easy way, i guess) and the thing still only takes 9 gallons max.  I've run completely out of gas from driving and taken i think a max of 9.1 gallons.  All i can figure is the pump can't suck the bottom gallon :P<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Devotshka (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||06-04-2001||07:18 PM||||The pumps rarely pull all of a tanks available volume. Even older cars with the pump on the motor could not suck a tank dry. <P>Part of this is intentional. It prevents the pump pulling water and trash off the bottom. Just how low you get depends allot on both how deep the pump sits and how the tank is shaped. A gallon of gas isn't very deep when it's spread over a large area.||||reg||1
